WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.900
Federal judge handing a

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:01.601
significant blow

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:02.435
to Donald Trump today,

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:03.970
rejecting the former president's

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:05.605
claims of executive privilege

00:00:05.605 --> 00:00:07.907
in that January six investigation.

00:00:08.241 --> 00:00:08.908
The judge

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:10.477
ordering a number of Trump's

00:00:10.477 --> 00:00:12.112
former top White House aides

00:00:12.112 --> 00:00:14.681
to testify before a grand jury.

00:00:14.681 --> 00:00:16.049
Sources tell CNN

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:17.584
the list includes

00:00:17.584 --> 00:00:18.885
former White House chief of staff

00:00:18.885 --> 00:00:19.853
Mark Meadows,

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:21.788
former director of national intelligence

00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:22.956
John Ratcliffe,

00:00:22.956 --> 00:00:24.190
former national security

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.693
adviser Robert O'Brien and others.

00:00:27.027 --> 00:00:27.794
Some of these aides

00:00:27.794 --> 00:00:28.962
have already testified,

00:00:28.962 --> 00:00:30.296
but now they're likely before

00:00:30.296 --> 00:00:31.331
forced to appear

00:00:31.331 --> 00:00:32.899
before the grand jury once again

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:34.567
for even more testimony

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:37.103
and ordered to turn over more documents.

00:00:37.137 --> 00:00:38.738
But as CNN's Jessica

00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:39.639
Stanford reports, Trump's

00:00:39.639 --> 00:00:41.074
legal team is expected

00:00:41.074 --> 00:00:44.544
to try to appeal this decision.

00:00:45.512 --> 00:00:47.213
Trump's closest advisers

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:49.215
ordered to testify in two Justice

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:50.216
Department probes.

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:50.984
A federal judge

00:00:50.984 --> 00:00:52.018
rejecting Trump's

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:53.686
claim of executive privilege,

00:00:53.686 --> 00:00:54.487
ordering former

00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:56.523
White House chief of Staff Mark Meadows,

00:00:56.523 --> 00:00:57.891
senior aide Stephen Miller

00:00:57.891 --> 00:00:59.526
and others to answer questions

00:00:59.526 --> 00:01:00.493
from a grand jury

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:01.795
investigating efforts

00:01:01.795 --> 00:01:03.663
to overturn the 2020 election.

00:01:04.764 --> 00:01:05.899
I remember leaning against

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:07.400
the doorway and saying,

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.735
it's an interesting conversation

00:01:08.735 --> 00:01:09.903
as pretty Mark.

00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:10.336
Sounds like

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:11.805
we're going to go to the Capitol.

00:01:11.805 --> 00:01:14.307
We're going to walk down to the Capitol.

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:16.109
He didn't look up from his phone

00:01:16.109 --> 00:01:18.645
and said something to the effect of,

00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:20.447
there's a lot going on, guys,

00:01:20.447 --> 00:01:22.482
but I don't know.

00:01:22.482 --> 00:01:24.150
Things might get real, real bad.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:24.851
Separately,

00:01:24.851 --> 00:01:26.219
Evan Corcoran, a top

00:01:26.219 --> 00:01:26.953
Trump attorney

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:28.788
and a crucial witness in special counsel

00:01:28.788 --> 00:01:30.957
Jack Smith's classified documents probe

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.525
spending nearly 4 hours

00:01:32.525 --> 00:01:34.160
testifying behind closed doors

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.162
to a federal grand jury on Friday.

00:01:36.162 --> 00:01:36.463
Trump

00:01:36.463 --> 00:01:37.530
also fought in court

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.165
to stop his testimony, but

00:01:39.165 --> 00:01:40.400
several judges ruling

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.502
Corcoran must divulge information

00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:43.670
about the conversations

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:45.405
he had with former President Trump

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:47.207
leading up to the FBI search of Mar

00:01:47.207 --> 00:01:48.408
a Lago last summer,

00:01:48.408 --> 00:01:50.243
and that Corcoran must turn over

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:51.077
handwritten notes

00:01:51.077 --> 00:01:53.012
documenting their interactions.

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:54.214
FBI agents seized

00:01:54.214 --> 00:01:56.049
more than 100 classified documents

00:01:56.049 --> 00:01:57.383
from Elago in August.

00:01:57.383 --> 00:01:58.952
They should give me immediately back

00:01:58.952 --> 00:02:00.420
everything that they've taken from me.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:01.921
Because it's mine. It's mine.

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:02.822
FBI agents

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:03.223
seized more

00:02:03.223 --> 00:02:04.924
than 100 classified documents

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:06.226
from Elago in August,

00:02:06.226 --> 00:02:07.260
and in November,

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:08.528
the attorney general appointed

00:02:08.528 --> 00:02:10.063
special counsel, Jack Smith,

00:02:10.063 --> 00:02:12.165
to investigate, among other things,

00:02:12.165 --> 00:02:13.466
whether Trump obstructed

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:15.001
the government's attempts to get back

00:02:15.001 --> 00:02:16.903
all of the classified material

00:02:16.903 --> 00:02:18.204
still in his possession

00:02:18.204 --> 00:02:19.506
after he left office.

00:02:19.506 --> 00:02:20.340
Evan Corcoran

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:21.774
crafted a statement in June

00:02:21.774 --> 00:02:23.443
20, 22 claiming

00:02:23.443 --> 00:02:24.244
a diligent

00:02:24.244 --> 00:02:26.079
search had been conducted at Mar a Lago

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:27.480
and that all classified

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:28.882
documents had been returned.

00:02:28.882 --> 00:02:30.016
A source tells CNN

00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:31.918
prosecutors wanted to ask Corcoran

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:33.019
about that statement

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:34.487
and a June phone call

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:36.422
between him and Trump that took place

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:38.491
the same day a subpoena was issued

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:39.392
for Mar a Lago.

00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:40.593
Surveillance footage

00:02:40.593 --> 00:02:41.728
that showed boxes

00:02:41.728 --> 00:02:43.596
being moved out of a storage room.

00:02:43.596 --> 00:02:46.432
You still have the surveillance tape,

00:02:46.432 --> 00:02:47.433
is that correct?

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:48.535
Absolutely, Sean.

00:02:48.535 --> 00:02:49.702
Sources tell CNN

00:02:49.702 --> 00:02:51.204
prosecutors have made clear

00:02:51.204 --> 00:02:52.205
that they believe Trump

00:02:52.205 --> 00:02:54.240
used Corcoran to advance a crime.

00:02:54.307 --> 00:02:55.942
A Trump spokesperson has fired

00:02:55.942 --> 00:02:57.777
back, accusing the Justice Department

00:02:57.777 --> 00:02:59.145
of continuously stepping

00:02:59.145 --> 00:03:00.947
far outside the standard norms

00:03:00.947 --> 00:03:01.648
in an attempt

00:03:01.648 --> 00:03:03.616
to destroy the long accepted,

00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:04.517
long held,

00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:05.885
constitutionally based

00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:07.220
standards of attorney client

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:09.255
privilege and executive privilege.

00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:10.256
From the beginning,

00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:12.192
he has tried to cooperate, Trump attorney

00:03:12.192 --> 00:03:13.993
Tim Palmer later tells CNN.

00:03:13.993 --> 00:03:15.328
He also testified

00:03:15.328 --> 00:03:16.863
before the grand jury in December,

00:03:16.863 --> 00:03:18.031
divulging details

00:03:18.031 --> 00:03:19.232
about additional searches

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:20.433
for classified documents

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:22.202
he organized at several Trump

00:03:22.202 --> 00:03:23.570
properties last year.

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:24.837
They would rather make this

00:03:24.837 --> 00:03:26.172
into an adversarial fight

00:03:26.172 --> 00:03:28.274
and try to make it into a criminal case.

00:03:29.342 --> 00:03:29.876
So Special

00:03:29.876 --> 00:03:30.443
Counsel Jack

00:03:30.443 --> 00:03:31.344
Smith will now be getting

00:03:31.344 --> 00:03:33.279
an influx of new information,

00:03:33.279 --> 00:03:34.414
both from Evan Corcoran,

00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:35.481
who was forced to testify

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:37.183
in front of the grand jury today,

00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:39.419
and also from that array of top Trump

00:03:39.419 --> 00:03:40.753
administration officials

00:03:40.753 --> 00:03:42.055
who will now have to testify

00:03:42.055 --> 00:03:43.022
to the grand jury

00:03:43.022 --> 00:03:43.456
about what

00:03:43.456 --> 00:03:45.992
transpired on and around January 6th.

00:03:45.992 --> 00:03:46.593
And, Jake.

00:03:46.593 --> 00:03:47.961
Our team is told that Trump's

00:03:47.961 --> 00:03:48.428
legal team

00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:50.630
is expected to appeal this decision

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:51.798
and not let these officials

00:03:51.798 --> 00:03:53.700
assert this executive privilege.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:54.567
And interestingly,

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:56.202
it does mean that some of these officials

00:03:56.202 --> 00:03:57.870
who have already been subpoenaed,

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:58.438
like the former

00:03:58.438 --> 00:03:59.105
national security

00:03:59.105 --> 00:04:00.173
adviser Robert O'Brien,

00:04:00.173 --> 00:04:01.374
also Ken Cuccinelli

00:04:01.374 --> 00:04:02.675
from Homeland Security.

00:04:02.675 --> 00:04:03.276
It's likely

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:05.979
they'll have to go back and testify more

00:04:05.979 --> 00:04:06.946
now that they can't assert

00:04:06.946 --> 00:04:07.647
that privilege.

00:04:07.647 --> 00:04:08.581
All right,

00:04:08.581 --> 00:04:09.816
Jessica Snider, thank you so much.

00:04:09.816 --> 00:04:11.384
Let's talk about this now

00:04:11.384 --> 00:04:12.619
with CNN senior legal

00:04:12.619 --> 00:04:13.386
analyst and former

00:04:13.386 --> 00:04:15.088
federal prosecutor Ali.

00:04:15.088 --> 00:04:16.389
Ali, a lot to talk about here.

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:19.892
Let's talk about the start with the judge

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:22.228
telling all these Trump people

00:04:22.228 --> 00:04:24.864
that they have to testify.

00:04:24.897 --> 00:04:26.099
Sources say Trump's legal team,

00:04:26.099 --> 00:04:27.233
they're expected to appeal.

00:04:27.233 --> 00:04:29.102
Do they have a case to make an executive

00:04:29.102 --> 00:04:30.003
privilege case?

00:04:31.371 --> 00:04:32.238
No, they don't, Jake.

00:04:32.238 --> 00:04:33.773
They certainly have the right to appeal,

00:04:33.773 --> 00:04:34.807
but they're not going to win.

00:04:34.807 --> 00:04:36.542
They've got two major problems.

00:04:36.542 --> 00:04:37.477
First of all, executive

00:04:37.477 --> 00:04:38.911
privilege is meant to design

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:39.812
to protect

00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:41.147
governmental conversations

00:04:41.147 --> 00:04:42.782
about legitimate policy,

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:43.983
legitimate strategy.

00:04:43.983 --> 00:04:45.952
It's not designed to cover up

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:47.787
for disclosure about malfeasance

00:04:47.787 --> 00:04:49.088
or wrongdoing or criminality.

00:04:49.088 --> 00:04:51.291
This is a criminal grand jury subpoena.

00:04:51.291 --> 00:04:52.425
Judges are very likely

00:04:52.425 --> 00:04:53.593
to enforce those subpoenas,

00:04:53.593 --> 00:04:54.694
very unlikely

00:04:54.694 --> 00:04:55.795
to allow an assertion

00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:56.896
of executive privilege.

00:04:56.896 --> 00:04:57.897
And second of all, Trump's

00:04:57.897 --> 00:04:58.931
a former president.

00:04:58.931 --> 00:05:00.533
And while a former president

00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:02.201
can exert executive privilege

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:04.237
in very narrow circumstances,

00:05:04.237 --> 00:05:06.572
it is a stark uphill climb to do that.

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:08.841
So Trump is also trying to assert

00:05:08.841 --> 00:05:11.311
executive privilege to stop

00:05:11.311 --> 00:05:12.345
Vice President Pence

00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:14.714
from being forced to testify.

00:05:14.747 --> 00:05:16.182
Do you think this ruling signals

00:05:16.182 --> 00:05:17.717
that that will also fail?

00:05:19.252 --> 00:05:20.253
I do think so, Jake.

00:05:20.253 --> 00:05:21.087
Yesterday on this show,

00:05:21.087 --> 00:05:23.156
I said that Trump had virtually no chance

00:05:23.156 --> 00:05:24.691
to succeed on the Mike Pence case.

00:05:24.691 --> 00:05:24.957
I guess

00:05:24.957 --> 00:05:26.926
I'll downgrade that even further now.

00:05:26.926 --> 00:05:28.328
But it's important to keep in mind

00:05:28.328 --> 00:05:29.462
there's a separate argument

00:05:29.462 --> 00:05:30.129
on the Pence case,

00:05:30.129 --> 00:05:32.165
because Mike Pence himself is arguing

00:05:32.432 --> 00:05:33.166
that he's protected

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:34.634
by a different legal doctrine,

00:05:34.634 --> 00:05:36.402
this idea of speech and debate,

00:05:36.402 --> 00:05:37.770
because as vice president,

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.372
he was president of the Senate.

00:05:39.372 --> 00:05:40.973
I think there's a better basis for that.

00:05:40.973 --> 00:05:42.375
And that might enable Pence

00:05:42.375 --> 00:05:43.476
to avoid answering

00:05:43.476 --> 00:05:44.410
some questions

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:45.778
about what he did in his role

00:05:45.778 --> 00:05:47.380
as Senate president.

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:49.382
If Trump loses these appeals,

00:05:49.382 --> 00:05:51.384
is there any way that Meadows

00:05:51.384 --> 00:05:52.719
and these other top Trump aides

00:05:52.719 --> 00:05:54.053
can get out of testifying?

00:05:54.053 --> 00:05:55.488
Can they plead the Fifth?

00:05:57.056 --> 00:05:57.557
There's only

00:05:57.557 --> 00:05:58.891
one way, and you just said it,

00:05:58.891 --> 00:06:00.126
they can take the Fifth.

00:06:00.126 --> 00:06:01.627
That means that any testimony

00:06:01.627 --> 00:06:03.363
they give might be used against

00:06:03.363 --> 00:06:04.597
and they have that absolute

00:06:04.597 --> 00:06:05.465
right to take the Fifth.

00:06:05.465 --> 00:06:08.067
But if they do that, DOJ prosecutors

00:06:08.101 --> 00:06:10.002
do have a countermove available.

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:11.137
They can go to a judge

00:06:11.137 --> 00:06:13.005
and ask for an order of immunity,

00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:14.540
meaning your testimony

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:16.275
is not going to be used against you,

00:06:16.275 --> 00:06:18.111
but now you have to testify.

00:06:18.111 --> 00:06:19.545
That's a strategic decision

00:06:19.545 --> 00:06:20.813
to be made by prosecutors.

00:06:20.813 --> 00:06:22.014
I made that kind of decision

00:06:22.014 --> 00:06:22.982
quite frequently,

00:06:22.982 --> 00:06:24.584
but it means they get the testimony.

00:06:24.584 --> 00:06:25.251
It also means

00:06:25.251 --> 00:06:26.119
they're very unlikely

00:06:26.119 --> 00:06:27.353
to be able to prosecute,

00:06:27.353 --> 00:06:28.354
whether it's Mark Meadows

00:06:28.354 --> 00:06:29.522
or whoever, the witnesses.

00:06:29.522 --> 00:06:30.656
So just a game this out.

00:06:30.656 --> 00:06:31.090
So let's say

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:32.992
I'm Mark Meadows and I say

00:06:32.992 --> 00:06:34.260
and I lose all the appeals

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:35.161
and I'm forced to testify.

00:06:35.161 --> 00:06:35.995
I say I'm going to assert

00:06:35.995 --> 00:06:36.562
my Fifth Amendment

00:06:36.562 --> 00:06:38.331
right against self-incrimination.

00:06:38.331 --> 00:06:39.699
And you're the prosecutor.

00:06:39.699 --> 00:06:40.633
You says

00:06:40.633 --> 00:06:42.335
we're giving you complete immunity.

00:06:42.335 --> 00:06:44.303
You won't be incriminating yourself.

00:06:44.303 --> 00:06:45.505
And then you go

00:06:45.505 --> 00:06:47.707
and then what do I do if I'm Mark Meadows

00:06:47.707 --> 00:06:49.142
and what do you do as the prosecutor?

00:06:50.643 --> 00:06:52.178
So this is not optional.

00:06:52.178 --> 00:06:54.180
If a prosecutor gives Mark Meadows

00:06:54.414 --> 00:06:56.282
immunity, he has to testify.

00:06:56.282 --> 00:06:56.883
It doesn't matter

00:06:56.883 --> 00:06:58.384
whether he wants immunity or not.

00:06:58.384 --> 00:06:59.619
And if he refuses,

00:06:59.619 --> 00:07:00.253
if he just says

00:07:00.253 --> 00:07:01.020
You can't force

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:02.455
the words out of my mouth,

00:07:02.455 --> 00:07:03.623
then we're in a contempt

00:07:03.623 --> 00:07:04.924
of court situation.

00:07:04.924 --> 00:07:06.659
And you may those who remember history

00:07:06.659 --> 00:07:07.994
may think of Susan McDougal,

00:07:07.994 --> 00:07:08.661
who was a witness

00:07:08.661 --> 00:07:09.929
in the Clinton investigation,

00:07:09.929 --> 00:07:11.397
the Whitewater investigation.

00:07:11.397 --> 00:07:13.433
She went to prison for 18 months

00:07:13.433 --> 00:07:14.700
for refusing to testify.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:16.202
So if it comes down to it,

00:07:16.202 --> 00:07:18.070
if DOJ says you're immunize meadows

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:19.772
and he says you can't make me talk,

00:07:19.772 --> 00:07:21.207
then a judge can absolutely

00:07:21.207 --> 00:07:22.675
throw Mark Meadows or whoever

00:07:22.675 --> 00:07:24.677
it is into prison until he does.

00:07:24.977 --> 00:07:26.012
Take us behind the scenes.

00:07:26.012 --> 00:07:26.946
If you're a prosecutor,

00:07:26.946 --> 00:07:28.381
you're about to question

00:07:28.381 --> 00:07:29.882
Meadows, Scavino, Miller,

00:07:29.882 --> 00:07:31.050
all these people who have had one

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:32.285
on one conversations

00:07:32.285 --> 00:07:33.953
with Donald Trump around January six.

00:07:33.953 --> 00:07:35.521
How are you preparing for that?

00:07:36.923 --> 00:07:37.890
Well, first of all, it's just

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:38.491
a goldmine

00:07:38.491 --> 00:07:39.725
to be able to go in and ask them.

00:07:39.725 --> 00:07:40.927
These are the key questions

00:07:40.927 --> 00:07:41.861
that you want to know.

00:07:41.861 --> 00:07:42.995
I'm looking at certainly

00:07:42.995 --> 00:07:43.996
all the documents.

00:07:43.996 --> 00:07:44.664
We know

00:07:44.664 --> 00:07:46.199
that prosecutors have sent out

00:07:46.199 --> 00:07:47.900
several documents for subpoenas.

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:48.835
I want to look at everything

00:07:48.835 --> 00:07:50.002
the January six committee did.

00:07:50.002 --> 00:07:51.037
And most importantly,

00:07:51.037 --> 00:07:52.104
we just heard a clip.

00:07:52.104 --> 00:07:53.105
Cassidy Hutchinson

00:07:53.105 --> 00:07:54.907
I would review every word

00:07:54.907 --> 00:07:56.108
that Cassidy Hutchinson said

00:07:56.108 --> 00:07:57.477
because she was one of Mark

00:07:57.477 --> 00:07:59.178
Meadows aides. She was by his side.

00:07:59.178 --> 00:08:00.346
She's already testified

00:08:00.346 --> 00:08:01.447
about conversations

00:08:01.447 --> 00:08:02.515
that she had with Meadows,

00:08:02.515 --> 00:08:03.416
but also conversations

00:08:03.416 --> 00:08:05.551
that she knows Meadows had with Trump.

00:08:05.551 --> 00:08:06.919
So I would go through that testimony

00:08:06.919 --> 00:08:07.553
very carefully

00:08:07.553 --> 00:08:08.988
and get out a red pen and circle

00:08:08.988 --> 00:08:10.923
all those pieces of testimony.

00:08:10.923 --> 00:08:11.824
Why is it taken

00:08:11.824 --> 00:08:13.493
so long to get this point?

00:08:13.493 --> 00:08:14.660
I mean, isn't Meadows

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:15.027
we know

00:08:15.027 --> 00:08:16.329
is that the White House in January six,

00:08:16.329 --> 00:08:18.231
one of the first witnesses you would have

00:08:18.231 --> 00:08:19.131
tried to secure?

00:08:20.566 --> 00:08:21.934
I completely agree

00:08:21.934 --> 00:08:23.269
with that sentiment, Jake.

00:08:23.269 --> 00:08:24.570
I think it's been a failure

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:25.404
by Merrick Garland

00:08:25.404 --> 00:08:26.339
that it's taken

00:08:26.339 --> 00:08:28.808
until mid 2023 for Mark Meadows

00:08:29.041 --> 00:08:30.209
to be compelled to testify.

00:08:30.209 --> 00:08:31.444
There was never any mystery.

00:08:31.444 --> 00:08:32.078
Mark Meadows

00:08:32.078 --> 00:08:32.812
and the others

00:08:32.812 --> 00:08:34.180
were at the center of this.

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:35.581
Garland has said all along,

00:08:35.581 --> 00:08:36.749
We start at the bottom

00:08:36.749 --> 00:08:37.717
and then we work our way up.

00:08:37.717 --> 00:08:39.452
But that's a bureaucratic approach.

00:08:39.452 --> 00:08:40.520
That's not a strong

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:42.054
prosecutorial approach.

00:08:42.054 --> 00:08:43.022
The reality is good

00:08:43.022 --> 00:08:45.024
prosecutors start at the highest point

00:08:45.157 --> 00:08:45.658
you can.

00:08:45.658 --> 00:08:46.626
And so I think an aggressive

00:08:46.626 --> 00:08:48.261
prosecutor on day one

00:08:48.261 --> 00:08:51.030
or perhaps a few months in in 2021

00:08:51.197 --> 00:08:52.698
would have immediately subpoenaed

00:08:52.698 --> 00:08:53.666
Mark Meadows

00:08:53.666 --> 00:08:55.067
and the other people we're talking about.

00:08:55.067 --> 00:08:55.968
And if that had happened,

00:08:55.968 --> 00:08:57.436
they would have had this testimony

00:08:57.436 --> 00:08:58.838
long ago.

00:08:58.838 --> 00:08:59.539
All right, Ellie Hoenig,

00:08:59.539 --> 00:09:00.006
thanks so much

00:09:00.006 --> 00:09:00.973
for your insights, as always.

